<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oleśnica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oleśnica is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. From around 1320 it was the capital of the Piast Duchy of Oleśnica, which later passed to the Poděbrady family and then to branches of the houses of Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle was their residence. Known in German as Oels, it became part of Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olesnica.jpg" alt="Oleśnica" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dawid Galus · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ole%C5%9Bnica_Zamek_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
